A||||7||sluppy123||? for an electrician||||1||
Z||000000||sluppy123||11-01-2000||11:15 PM||||I'm buying a Milling machine with a 3-phase motor on it and my house is only supplied with single phase power. My question is do I use a Phase converter or change out the motor?  What kind of converter do you recomend? <P>What If I were to buy a 440 volt, 3 phase Machine but my house is supplied with 220, What do I do in this case?<P>Thanks for any help.<P><P>------------------<BR>-Brian S. <BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>85' SC & 87' GT-Leather, Lowered and On The Bottle||||reg||1
Z||000001||hugh||11-02-2000||07:15 AM||||How many horsepower is the motor driving the machine?Your best bet would be to replace the motor with a single phase 220 volt motor,if it is belt drive you can adjust pulley size to use a smaller motor,it will slow it down but it can be made to operate within the amperage supply ability of your building.You don't want it to make your lights blink when it starts.||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||11-02-2000||07:56 AM||||Sluppy,<P>I am coming to your house!  I love turning handles when the product is for MY car.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-02-2000||09:03 PM||||Well..... You likely won't be able to get 3 phase in a residence. I think it's actually illegal in many states.<P>You're pretty much going to have to do something with the motor. You might be able to get it rewound or rewired. Just which is dependant on the motor. Those options are usually cheaper than new motors.<P>The converter/transformer options will be very expencive and also may violate residential electrical codes. Plus your service entrance is likely only a 100-200 amp setup which probably will not be strong enough. Even if the service entrance can take the load you'll have to run dedicated circuits from the breaker box. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||hugh||11-02-2000||09:46 PM||||I use motors in my work,If you let me know what you have,that is,Frame number and Horsepower,if pulley driven,what size pulley.I may be able to suggest a suitable replacement   ||||reg||1
Z||000005||sluppy123||11-03-2000||06:54 PM||||Thanks for the info. I'll keep you posted. <P>Can someone give me the low down on how a static phase converter works? why wouldn't this work for my application?<P><BR>(BTW it's only a 1 hp. motor)<BR>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>-Brian S. <BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>85' SC & 87' GT-Leather, Lowered and On The Bottle||||reg||3
Z||000006||hugh||11-03-2000||10:25 PM||||I don't use phase converters,but what they  do is change your single phase 230vac to a power supply your motor can use.I won't be a true 3 phase as supplied by the power co.,but a motor can run on it without any negative effects.If you only have a 1 hp motor,I don't think it would be cost effective to use any converter because of the added power consumption.Converters are not 100% efficient and will actually increase your energy use.A home with 100 amp service should be able to handle a 1 hp motor with no problem,it needs it's own circuit though.200 amp service is even better.One last thing ,a 1 hp single phase motor would probably cost less than a phase converter.One more last thing,3 phase motors are 1750 rpm,If you get a motor make sure it is 1725/1750 rpm.A reverseable motor is best so you can spin it the right direction.All this typing made me thirsty,I'm going to get myself a beer.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||11-04-2000||08:24 PM||||Only a 1 horse..... have it rewound or replace it. Either is likely cheaper than adding a converter.<P>You can run the one horse in a house. absolutly must have it's own circuit run tho.||||reg||1
